1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
We'll toast St. Patrick in Dublin and have a dram in Denver.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
And we'll find out what makes Scotch's single, Tennessee whiskey's straight, and Canadian's

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:11,000
great mixers.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Whether you call it moonshine, bourbon, or even the water of life, it's a real liquid

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:17,000
asset.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
We have bottles in here that will fetch $500 a glass.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, the untold story of whiskey on Modern Marbles.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,760
A fire rages at the Jack Daniel Distillery in Lynchburg, Tennessee.

9
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:47,940
But rather than dampen it, workers stoke it with an unusual fuel.

10
00:00:47,941 --> 00:00:56,780
We're using 140 proof whiskey to start the fire because if we use gasoline or diesel or anything like that, it'll contaminate the charcoal.

11
00:00:56,781 --> 00:00:59,400
Each one of these stacks of wood is called a rick.

12
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:02,320
It takes 16 ricks to fill a vat of charcoal.

13
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,900
To remove impurities from their whiskey,

14
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:09,040
distillers at Jack Daniel's filter every drop of it

15
00:01:09,041 --> 00:01:12,200
through vats of sugar maple charcoal they manufacture on site.

16
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,060
This process is the one thing that we do,

17
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:18,760
manufacturing Jack Daniel's, that distinguishes us from a bourbon.

18
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,880
This lone flavor modification makes Jack Daniel's

19
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:24,700
a Tennessee straight whiskey and not a bourbon.

20
00:01:24,701 --> 00:01:31,700
Bourbon, made with a majority of corn, can't have any flavor modification.

21
00:01:31,701 --> 00:01:35,700
It's just distillate and water, aged in oak barrels.

22
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:39,700
The whiskey category is ripe with these kinds of subtle distinctions.

23
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:48,700
There are Tennessee whiskeys, bourbons, Irish whiskeys, Canadian whiskeys, scotches, ryes, even a Colorado whiskey.

24
00:01:48,701 --> 00:01:52,700
Though distinct, they're all whiskeys because they have one thing in common.

25
00:01:52,701 --> 00:01:58,500
Well, the main thing that differentiates whiskey from the other spirits in the world is the fact that whiskeys are made from grain.

26
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:05,420
A major grain producer, the United States is also the world's number two producer of whiskey behind Scotland.

27
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,040
Canada, Japan and Ireland follow.

28
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:12,940
Whiskey is the world's second most popular distilled spirit after vodka.

29
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:16,220
And the U.S. consumes more of it than anyone else.

30
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:20,140
Jack Daniels holds the title of the world's number one brand of whiskey.

31
00:02:20,141 --> 00:02:23,260
selling almost 24 million gallons per year.

32
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:29,640
Most American whiskeys like Jack Daniels fall into a subgroup called straight whiskey.

33
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:35,800
To be designated a straight whiskey, a manufacturer must follow tight governmental guidelines.

34
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:41,680
When you're making a straight whiskey, it can only be distilled to no more than 160 proof

35
00:02:41,681 --> 00:02:46,060
and it can't go into a barrel no more than 125 proof

36
00:02:46,061 --> 00:02:51,900
and it has to be aged in new charred oak barrels for a minimum of two years.

37
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:57,460
And on top of that, each style of straight whiskey has to have a major grain component.

38
00:02:57,461 --> 00:03:02,420
What makes one whiskey different from another is the type of grains that are used,

39
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:05,460
the way that they're distilled, and how they're aged.

40
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:11,000
These differences lead to distinct flavor profiles, like that of Jack Daniels.

41
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:15,540
Ethan Kelly is a spirit sommelier, a trained expert in the hard stuff.

42
00:03:15,541 --> 00:03:20,980
At the Brandy Library in New York City, he oversees one of the country's most extensive whiskey collections.

43
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:23,760
Ethan gives Jack a taste test.

44
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:32,200
When you notice this one, the wood does pop right out, and it is clear that you're in a forest.

45
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:37,040
You do get some very earthy qualities to it, almost farmhouse qualities.

46
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:42,480
It's fatty, and it's earthy, and it's sweet.

47
00:03:42,481 --> 00:03:48,820
That farmhouse quality comes from, well, the farm.

48
00:03:48,821 --> 00:03:56,440
Jack Daniels starts with a recipe, also known as a grain bill, of 80% corn, 12% barley, and

49
00:03:56,441 --> 00:03:59,840
8% rye.

50
00:03:59,841 --> 00:04:05,120
The distillery, located on this site since the 1860s, has its own water source that also

51
00:04:05,121 --> 00:04:06,720
influences flavor.

52
00:04:06,721 --> 00:04:13,720
Jack Daniel purchased the whiskey making operation from a Lutheran minister in his early teens,

53
00:04:13,721 --> 00:04:18,720
but he located the manufacturing operation here because of an abundant limestone water.

54
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:24,720
It's an iron-free water source that flows out of the cave at a constant 56 degrees year-round.

55
00:04:24,721 --> 00:04:28,720
This lack of iron affects the fermentation process.

56
00:04:28,721 --> 00:04:35,920
yeast likes to have all the metals present a balanced diet so what we've done is by supplying

57
00:04:35,921 --> 00:04:41,440
it with iron-free water we tend to shift the fermentation just a little bit and that shift is

58
00:04:41,441 --> 00:04:46,720
part of what makes the octanel the grain and water are pumped into what's known as the mash

59
00:04:46,721 --> 00:04:53,820
cooker and cooked into a runny porridge temperatures of up to 210 degrees break down the walls of the

60
00:04:53,821 --> 00:04:57,580
grain, allowing water to soak the starches.

61
00:04:57,581 --> 00:05:00,780
Enzymes in the grain can then convert starches into sugars,

62
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:03,860
readying them for fermentation.

63
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:07,120
This mash is then transferred into 40,000 gallon fermenting

64
00:05:07,121 --> 00:05:10,360
vats, where for the next three to five days,

65
00:05:10,361 --> 00:05:14,120
yeast goes to work making alcohol.

66
00:05:14,121 --> 00:05:16,140
All the agitation that you see here

67
00:05:16,141 --> 00:05:19,060
is strictly from the yeast acting on the fermentable

68
00:05:19,061 --> 00:05:20,020
sugars.

69
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:23,520
And they start converting fermentable sugars to CO2,

70
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:29,040
ethanol so when I see a bubble of co2 in here I've also got a bubble of ethanol

71
00:05:29,041 --> 00:05:33,540
that I've made ethanol the alcohol that the yeast converts from the green

72
00:05:33,541 --> 00:05:39,420
sugars reaches about 10% concentration by the end of fermentation this

73
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:44,700
alcoholic mash is now known as distiller's beer the stillers beer is a

74
00:05:44,701 --> 00:05:49,480
little different than normal beer in that we ferment the whole grain what

75
00:05:49,481 --> 00:05:53,980
What we're doing here is we're pumping the distiller's beer to the top of the beer's

76
00:05:53,981 --> 00:05:57,440
still at about 250 gallons a minute.

77
00:05:57,441 --> 00:05:59,400
We add the steam in the bottom of the still.

78
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:02,800
When we do that, then the steam goes up through the column.

79
00:06:02,801 --> 00:06:06,160
There's a series of what we call stripping trays in here.

80
00:06:06,161 --> 00:06:10,560
Those stripping trays have several hundred holes in them.

81
00:06:10,561 --> 00:06:15,620
The column of steam collides with the distiller's beer, vaporizing the more volatile alcohol

82
00:06:15,621 --> 00:06:18,440
which passes out the top of the still.

83
00:06:18,441 --> 00:06:23,000
The perforated trays allow the grain husks and water that had been stripped of alcohol

84
00:06:23,001 --> 00:06:27,200
to flow out of the bottom of the still.

85
00:06:27,201 --> 00:06:29,500
The final distillate is a clear grain alcohol.

86
00:06:29,501 --> 00:06:34,820
Now this is 140 proof and there's about 40 gallons a minute coming out of here.

87
00:06:34,821 --> 00:06:41,040
I've heard this called white dog, white mule, white lightning.

88
00:06:41,041 --> 00:06:45,420
It's just new whiskey.

89
00:06:45,421 --> 00:06:50,660
Every drop of this new 140 proof whiskey will be filtered through one of 72 charcoal mellowing

90
00:06:50,661 --> 00:06:58,000
vats, producing as much as 80,000 gallons of filtered whiskey per day.

91
00:06:58,001 --> 00:07:02,880
It takes the whiskey approximately one day to work its way down towards the bottom of

92
00:07:02,881 --> 00:07:04,200
the charcoal.

93
00:07:04,201 --> 00:07:12,000
This charcoal has very strong absorptive capabilities to bind some of the impurities that come off

94
00:07:12,001 --> 00:07:14,100
in the new whiskey through distillation.

95
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:22,420
Workers then pump the whiskey into new charred white oak barrels.

96
00:07:22,421 --> 00:07:30,420
Each barrel weighs in at 500 pounds and must be loaded into the maturation warehouse by hand.

97
00:07:30,421 --> 00:07:35,420
Easily a good entry day is about a thousand barrels.

98
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:40,420
Just depends on, a lot of it depends on how the house is rolling.

99
00:07:40,421 --> 00:07:43,860
It depends on, a lot of it depends on how the house is rolling.

