Jameson, founded in 1780, like Dublin,

228
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:35,160
is in the midst of its own resurgence.

229
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:44,840
Ten years ago, we would have distilled somewhere in the order of maybe 12 million litres of pure alcohol per annum.

230
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:47,640
Currently, we're distilling 25 million litres.

231
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:51,420
It's a very exciting time to be involved in the Jemson story in Ireland.

232
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:55,920
Part of this excitement is coming from reaching a new, younger customer.

233
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:58,540
It would have been the case, not too many years ago,

234
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,160
that they would have regarded whiskey as maybe an older person's beverage,

235
00:17:02,161 --> 00:17:04,160
and that is not the case with Jameson.

236
00:17:04,161 --> 00:17:08,160
Jameson's smooth taste profile is key to its growing appeal.

237
00:17:08,161 --> 00:17:12,160
The best whiskeys evolve on the palate as you're tasting it.

238
00:17:12,161 --> 00:17:14,160
And this is the Jameson 18-year-old,

239
00:17:14,161 --> 00:17:18,160
and this is an outstanding blended Irish whiskey.

240
00:17:18,161 --> 00:17:24,160
It's a nice silky texture to it, lots of toffee and fruit,

241
00:17:24,161 --> 00:17:28,160
a little bit of nuttiness, some of that cereal grain.

242
00:17:28,161 --> 00:17:33,660
cereal grain, it's continuing to evolve, fading on to a nice, soft, malty finish.

243
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:39,660
This accessible yet complex blended whiskey is produced by an Irish whiskey industry that

244
00:17:39,661 --> 00:17:41,495
is highly consolidated.

245
00:17:41,496 --> 00:17:46,600
The island has only three major distilleries, Bushmills, Cooley,

246
00:17:46,601 --> 00:17:51,286
and Middleton, but they produce roughly 30 different whiskeys.

247
00:17:51,287 --> 00:17:53,100
Jameson's old distillery

248
00:17:53,101 --> 00:17:59,260
in middleton dating back to the 1820s is now a visitor center and serves as a graceful facade

249
00:17:59,261 --> 00:18:04,300
for the very industrial guts of the operation the working distillery built in the 1970s

250
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:11,580
two grains fuel this concrete and steel behemoth corn distilled into a pure grain alcohol almost

251
00:18:11,581 --> 00:18:19,260
like a vodka and for flavor barley it gives it a very smooth flavor on the palate and it also gives

252
00:18:19,261 --> 00:18:26,920
it a like a silky and serum-y mouthfeel and that's what the barley does in the mash.

253
00:18:26,921 --> 00:18:31,700
To provide the sugars necessary for fermentation, the barley has been soaked in water in order

254
00:18:31,701 --> 00:18:34,260
to induce it to malt or germinate.

255
00:18:34,261 --> 00:18:38,880
What you're doing is really fooling the barley grain into thinking it's going to grow up

256
00:18:38,881 --> 00:18:40,480
into a barley plant.

257
00:18:40,481 --> 00:18:46,700
So you're allowing it to access the starchy reservoir within the grain.

258
00:18:46,701 --> 00:18:50,360
Once these sugars have been extracted from the malted barley in the brew house, they

259
00:18:50,361 --> 00:18:54,040
are pumped into gigantic fermentation vats.

260
00:18:54,041 --> 00:19:02,340
Up here we have our fermentation vats, 32 in total, each containing 200,000 liters of

261
00:19:02,341 --> 00:19:06,060
fermenting beer or fermenting wash.

262
00:19:06,061 --> 00:19:11,140
Yeast is added and it is here that the yeast will convert with that rich sugary liquid

263
00:19:11,141 --> 00:19:17,460
into alcohol and also into a whole range of fantastic flavors.

264
00:19:17,461 --> 00:19:19,960
This is the still house of the Jamison Distillery.

265
00:19:19,961 --> 00:19:22,540
Behind me here we have the first pot still.

266
00:19:22,541 --> 00:19:27,260
The alcoholic vapors, along with all of the volatile flavor compounds,

267
00:19:27,261 --> 00:19:29,180
rise up through the neck of the still,

268
00:19:29,181 --> 00:19:33,180
across the line arm, and gets condensed back into a liquid again.

269
00:19:33,181 --> 00:19:35,640
This liquid is known as low wines,

270
00:19:35,641 --> 00:19:39,280
and it contains about 20 to 40 percent alcohol by volume.

271
00:19:39,281 --> 00:19:42,040
This low wine is then pumped into the second pot still here.

272
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:43,860
Again, heat is applied.

273
00:19:44,220 --> 00:19:46,840
The alcoholic vapors rise up through the neck of the still.

274
00:19:47,440 --> 00:19:51,420
The strength at this stage has been increased to about 70% alcohol by volume

275
00:19:51,421 --> 00:19:54,320
is then taken and put into the third still.

276
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:59,620
Triple distillation has always been type of distillation used in Ireland.

277
00:19:59,621 --> 00:20:02,800
And that's because it produces a particular character

278
00:20:02,801 --> 00:20:05,020
and a particular style of distillate.

279
00:20:05,021 --> 00:20:15,576
What it really does is that it removes some of the heaviness to allow you to push the flavor spectrum more into the floral and fruity, spicy and fragrant

280
00:20:15,577 --> 00:20:16,680
characteristics.

281
00:20:17,320 --> 00:20:22,040
The Jameson blend is a combination of these pot still whiskeys and lighter grain whiskey.

282
00:20:23,060 --> 00:20:29,940
Grain whiskey, it's essentially from corn, what we call maize, distilled in column stills.

283
00:20:29,941 --> 00:20:33,380
so it comes off the still at quite a high alcoholic strength,

284
00:20:33,680 --> 00:20:35,460
and it's got a lighter style.

285
00:20:36,980 --> 00:20:39,620
This lighter whiskey is aged in bourbon barrels

286
00:20:39,621 --> 00:20:41,740
that have already been used several times.

287
00:20:47,060 --> 00:20:51,060
Now here we see inside of what a bourbon barrel looks like.

288
00:20:52,680 --> 00:20:54,280
Whiskey barrels are porous.

289
00:20:54,880 --> 00:20:57,060
In the summer months, as the whiskey warms up,

290
00:20:57,580 --> 00:20:59,020
pressure builds inside the barrel.

291
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:04,400
The whiskey penetrates the oak, absorbing both the wood's flavors and its amber color.

292
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:10,720
It's more than just a maturation, but it's almost like a melding of flavors.

293
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:14,500
The charred oak is saturated with 20 pounds of bourbon.

294
00:21:15,320 --> 00:21:17,740
Both oak and bourbon contribute to Jameson's flavor.

295
00:21:18,380 --> 00:21:23,620
We don't use any virgin oak in our whiskey production

296
00:21:23,621 --> 00:21:27,800
because the heavy extracts from the virgin oak

297
00:21:27,801 --> 00:21:32,120
would be too heavy for the light distillate that we produce here in Middleton.

298
00:21:33,620 --> 00:21:36,520
Pot still whiskey is also aged in a refill oak barrel.

299
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:39,160
It's one that's been used two or three times.

300
00:21:39,880 --> 00:21:42,080
And the whiskey that comes out of such a barrel

301
00:21:42,081 --> 00:21:46,080
allows more of the distillate character to be retained.

302
00:21:47,840 --> 00:21:51,580
But Jameson ages some of its whiskey in sherry casks,

303
00:21:51,581 --> 00:21:54,620
which are two and a half times bigger than standard oak barrels.

304
00:21:55,180 --> 00:22:00,660
You can take that same whiskey and mature it in a sherry cask.

305
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,980
And you can see here that the color is quite different.

306
00:22:03,760 --> 00:22:09,080
And that's because Spanish oak has a higher level of oak extract and tannin.

307
00:22:09,620 --> 00:22:13,960
The sherry butts are very much different from our bourbon barrel.

308
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:19,120
They're seasoned with sherry wine for two and a half years

309
00:22:19,121 --> 00:22:22,480
before they come home to be used here in the distillery.

310
00:22:22,740 --> 00:22:26,720
The sherry gives you a nutty taste, honey notes,

311
00:22:26,721 --> 00:22:31,340
gives you a nice, deep, chestnutty colour to the spirit.

312
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:38,540
In our warehouses here in Middleton, we would have circa 650,000 casks.

313
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:44,280
We are filling approximately 175,000 casks a year,

314
00:22:44,281 --> 00:22:49,440
and we're dumping about 130,000 casks a year.

315
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:54,360
But not all of the whiskey that goes into the barrels will make it into a bottle

316
00:22:54,361 --> 00:22:56,900
because of what's known as the angel's share.

317
00:22:57,380 --> 00:23:02,100
When the alcohol is expanding, it's pushing out into the yolk and evaporating off.

318
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:09,260
So we lose about 2.2, 2.3% per annum to the angel's share.

319
00:23:09,261 --> 00:23:16,260
This most modern and sophisticated of products has roots in Irish monasteries, like this

320
00:23:16,860 --> 00:23:19,677
one at Glindalough in County Wicklow.

321
00:23:19,678 --> 00:23:23,560
Irish monks made whisky at sites like these perhaps

322
00:23:23,561 --> 00:23:26,560
as early as a thousand years ago.

323
00:23:26,561 --> 00:23:28,512
Whisky was invented here in Ireland.

324
00:23:28,513 --> 00:23:31,820
Missionary monks from Ireland went out to preach Christianity

325
00:23:31,821 --> 00:23:32,644
in Europe.

326
00:23:32,645 --> 00:23:38,820
They discovered in the east an alembic, a pot still, a distillation vessel.

327
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:41,820
and they brought this back to Ireland.

328
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:43,700
It had been used for perfume.

329
00:23:44,120 --> 00:23:46,060
They found a much better use for it,

330
00:23:46,420 --> 00:23:48,800
and they began to distill whiskey.

331
00:23:49,380 --> 00:23:52,580
Whiskey, they called it with the Gaelic name Ishgabaha.

332
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:55,120
Ishgabaha, the water of life.

333
00:23:56,540 --> 00:23:58,560
Today, Ishgabaha, or whiskey,

334
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:02,160
is being produced in quantities the Irish monks could never have imagined.

335
00:24:03,560 --> 00:24:06,060
At Jameson, once the whiskey has matured,

336
00:24:06,220 --> 00:24:08,860
the finished product is shipped from the distillery in Middleton.

337
00:24:09,260 --> 00:24:10,760
to Dublin for bottling.

338
00:24:10,761 --> 00:24:12,200
Here at the Jamison Bottling Plant,

339
00:24:12,201 --> 00:24:15,580
we're running all week, every week of the year.

340
00:24:15,581 --> 00:24:18,440
We're producing about a million bottles a week,

341
00:24:18,441 --> 00:24:21,240
in total between 40 and 50 million bottles every year.

342
00:24:24,940 --> 00:24:27,260
In Ireland, a single distillery produces

343
00:24:27,261 --> 00:24:30,380
a variety of whiskeys for its own blends.

344
00:24:30,381 --> 00:24:32,760
But in Scotland, competing distilleries

345
00:24:32,761 --> 00:24:34,560
have developed a surprising system

346
00:24:34,561 --> 00:24:36,180
to achieve their complex blends.

347
00:24:36,181 --> 00:24:44,320
The Buena Vista Cafe in San Francisco is the largest consumer of Irish whiskey in the United States.

348
00:24:44,980 --> 00:24:47,460
It serves about 2,000 Irish coffees every day.

349
00:24:48,140 --> 00:24:51,140
A mixture of hot coffee, Irish whiskey, sugar, and cream.

350
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:55,040
Whiskey will return on Modern Marvels.

351
00:24:57,900 --> 00:25:02,300
Scotland is a small country with a giant presence in the global whiskey industry.

352
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:07,360
It has roughly 90 distilleries, more than any other whiskey-producing country on earth.

353
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:13,380
And whiskey is Scotland's largest export, grossing almost $6 billion per year.

354
00:25:14,580 --> 00:25:19,820
It was global interest in Scotland's single malt scotches in the 1980s and 1990s

355
00:25:19,821 --> 00:25:23,300
that revived a whiskey category that had been in decline for decades.

356
00:25:24,420 --> 00:25:28,780
Scotland's single malt scotches are some of the most expensive, sought-after whiskeys in the world.

357
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:38,680
Single means it's the product of just one distillery.

358
00:25:39,680 --> 00:25:42,420
Malt means that it's made exclusively for malted barley.

359
00:25:43,340 --> 00:25:49,880
And scotch means it has to be distilled and matured in scowl and in oak barrels for a minimum of three years.

360
00:25:50,540 --> 00:25:55,340
Here at the Glenlivet Distillery, we only produce one whiskey, and that is the Glenlivet single malt.

361
00:25:55,940 --> 00:25:58,840
The Glenlivet is the world's number two single malt whiskey.

362
00:25:58,841 --> 00:26:06,940
A single malt like the Glenlivet may be sold on its own, or as part of a blended whisky, like Chivas Regal.

363
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:13,520
Despite Scotland's reputation for single malts, 90% of its whiskies are sold as blends,

364
00:26:13,920 --> 00:26:16,280
which are more accessible to the tastes of the average consumer.

365
00:26:17,380 --> 00:26:22,000
When we create a blended whisky, you have to use about 30 single malts.

366
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:27,660
So we're constantly exchanging whiskies with some of our competitor companies.

367
00:26:28,840 --> 00:26:33,100
Most Scottish distilleries only distill one scotch, their own single malt.

368
00:26:34,020 --> 00:26:37,800
As a result, they must rely on other distilleries to contribute scotches to their blend.

369
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:41,360
There's a great deal of diversity to Scotch whiskeys.

370
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:44,860
There are four recognized whiskey-making regions.

371
00:26:45,980 --> 00:26:49,280
Firstly, in the south of Scotland, an area known as the Lowlands.

372
00:26:49,780 --> 00:26:55,340
And the whiskies in the Lowlands tend to be very soft and gentle in their characteristic.

373
00:26:55,341 --> 00:26:59,560
Then we have a little island on the west coast of Scotland known as Islay.

374
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:05,660
And the whiskeys from Islay are very smoky, very pungent, very distinctive.

375
00:27:07,560 --> 00:27:10,340
The west coast whiskeys get their smoke from peat.

376
00:27:10,940 --> 00:27:16,600
The distilleries on the west coast traditionally have always used peat to dry their malted barley.

377
00:27:16,900 --> 00:27:20,180
And when you burn the peat, it gives off a very distinctive smoke.

378
00:27:20,181 --> 00:27:25,280
This clings to the malted barley and in turn it flavours the whisky.

379
00:27:25,540 --> 00:27:29,600
Then we have a huge geographic region known as the Highlands

380
00:27:29,601 --> 00:27:35,440
and the malt whiskies from the Highlands tend to be fruity and spicy in their characteristic.

381
00:27:36,460 --> 00:27:40,620
Here in Speyside, the greatest of all of the four whisky making regions,

382
00:27:41,060 --> 00:27:46,220
the whiskies from Speyside tend to be sweet and floral and fruity in their characteristics.

383
00:27:46,221 --> 00:27:52,420
Hundreds of years ago, the Speyside region was known as a haven for illegal distillers.

384
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:59,840
After the Battle of Culloden in 1746, where the English heavily defeated the Scots,

385
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:07,140
the English taxed the making of Scotch whisky to such an extent that the Scots couldn't make it legally.

386
00:28:07,700 --> 00:28:09,900
So they all began making it illegally.

387
00:28:10,900 --> 00:28:15,200
They used portable stills like this one that could be moved if soldiers showed up.

388
00:28:15,201 --> 00:28:18,180
This is the size of still that you'd have saw the illegal distillers using.

389
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:21,440
It would have been filled with that mixture of sweet liquids,

390
00:28:21,720 --> 00:28:24,560
the wort that they used as the first stage of the process.

391
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:26,660
It would have been boiled up inside here,

392
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:29,720
and then you'd have watched the vapors coming over here.

393
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:31,680
As it goes down through the coil in the cold water,

394
00:28:31,780 --> 00:28:33,820
it's going to condense back into a liquid again

395
00:28:33,821 --> 00:28:36,340
before appearing out the funnel at the end.

396
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:41,840
Today, water is still key to the operation of the distillery.

397
00:28:41,841 --> 00:28:46,840
We use probably in the region of 3 million litres of water a day in between mashing,

398
00:28:46,841 --> 00:28:48,840
cooling, condensing.

399
00:28:48,841 --> 00:28:54,840
The distillery also consumes 40 tonnes of malted or germinated barley a day.

400
00:28:54,841 --> 00:28:57,840
Glenlivet is made from 100% barley.

401
00:28:57,841 --> 00:28:59,840
And this is the mash tonne.

402
00:28:59,841 --> 00:29:04,840
And this is where we start to extract the sugars and the starches that we're going to make into alcohol.

403
00:29:04,841 --> 00:29:09,840
And the way we do that is we fill 12 tonnes of grist into this vessel.

404
00:29:11,840 --> 00:29:17,500
Hot water extracts the sugars, and then this liquid, the wort, is pumped into the fermenters.

405
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:23,560
Into each of these, 60,000 liters of liquid will come through from the mash tun,

406
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:30,540
and then we will add 215 kilos of yeast, and that creates a 100% natural process.

407
00:29:31,060 --> 00:29:32,480
So this is where we create the alcohol.

408
00:29:33,820 --> 00:29:38,060
After two days, the 8.5% beer is ready for double distillation.

409
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:41,520
That's very similar to Jameson's triple distillation process.

410
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:48,840
Surprisingly, the entire distillery is monitored by only two people and banks of computers.

411
00:29:48,841 --> 00:29:54,840
Though computers have a large hand in making it, the Glenlipid has a sensual appeal.

412
00:29:58,840 --> 00:30:03,840
Wonderfully soft, wonderfully rounded and rich on the nose.

413
00:30:03,841 --> 00:30:04,640
on the nose.

414
00:30:08,800 --> 00:30:10,780
And it just is beautiful.

415
00:30:10,980 --> 00:30:14,880
It just goes down so gently, warming all the way.

416
00:30:15,920 --> 00:30:18,620
On a cold morning here at Glenlivet Distillery,

417
00:30:19,220 --> 00:30:21,460
this is central heating as it should be.

418
00:30:23,540 --> 00:30:26,600
Single malt scotches are the world's most expensive whiskeys.

419
00:30:27,540 --> 00:30:30,560
Glenlivet's range from their 12-year-old, which costs $35,

420
00:30:30,561 --> 00:30:35,160
to the Glenlivet 25-year-old, which will set you back $350,

421
00:30:35,700 --> 00:30:39,600
to the Glenlivet Cellar Collection 1964 Limited Edition,

422
00:30:40,080 --> 00:30:43,080
with only 1,824 bottles produced,

423
00:30:43,700 --> 00:30:46,920
which goes for a tidy $2,000 a bottle.

424
00:30:48,880 --> 00:30:51,740
And scotch prices for rare bottles just go up from there.

425
00:30:52,380 --> 00:30:54,920
One of the world's most expensive bottles of whiskey,

426
00:30:54,921 --> 00:30:57,400
a 1926 Macallan scotch,

427
00:30:57,401 --> 00:31:01,540
was purchased for $75,000 in Seoul, South Korea.

428
00:31:02,500 --> 00:31:05,240
How does a bottle of scotch command this kind of price tag?

429
00:31:06,140 --> 00:31:09,080
Rarity, number one, is remarkably important.

430
00:31:09,620 --> 00:31:11,440
Are you drinking, in fact, a piece of history?

431
00:31:12,620 --> 00:31:16,240
Two, is the historical significance.

432
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:21,160
If we're going to drink that old, did this define a category or did it not?

433
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:26,300
Why are people buying these $300, $400, $500 and up whiskeys?

434
00:31:26,301 --> 00:31:34,040
It's a lot easier to go out and buy a $300 bottle of whiskey than it is a $40,000 sports car or a $600,000 vacation home.

435
00:31:34,460 --> 00:31:37,760
You know, it's an affordable luxury. It's a beautiful thing.

436
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:43,040
Though Scotch whiskey distillers historically had their struggles with the law,

437
00:31:43,560 --> 00:31:49,180
in this country just north of the United States, distillers flourished because of the law, Prohibition.

438
00:31:51,420 --> 00:31:55,840
Scotland produces over 750 million liters of whiskey each year.

439
00:31:56,300 --> 00:32:00,660
That's more than three bottles of scotch for every man, woman, and child in the United States.

440
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:04,560
Whiskey will return on Modern Marvels.

441
00:32:05,920 --> 00:32:10,240
A country's most prized products often reflect the personality of its people.

442
00:32:11,540 --> 00:32:15,180
Canadian whiskeys, mostly blends of rye and neutral grain spirits,

443
00:32:15,620 --> 00:32:18,740
underscore the easy nature of both Canada and its whiskey.

444
00:32:18,741 --> 00:32:28,720
Canadian whiskey is probably the lightest and most delicate and easiest to embrace whiskey of all the major whiskey-producing countries.

445
00:32:30,580 --> 00:32:37,480
Canada's distillers, the world's number three producers of whiskey, make almost 48 million gallons of whiskey per year.

446
00:32:40,700 --> 00:32:47,560
The Canadian national style originated in 1884 with the introduction of one of the country's best-known whiskeys,

447
00:32:47,561 --> 00:32:53,920
Canadian Club. The Canadian Club classic 12-year-old is the company's most mature

448
00:32:53,921 --> 00:32:59,980
expression of its flavor profile. What we do get is vanilla and lots of it.

449
00:32:59,981 --> 00:33:08,080
We do get some of that grainy character from the rye. That is all the way through

450
00:33:08,081 --> 00:33:13,300
an enjoyable pleasant drink.

451
00:33:13,301 --> 00:33:18,820
The likeness of Canadian Club comes from its base whiskey, a corn distillate with no color.

